Several of the quarter designs were taken from or inspired by previous Mint releases. The reverse of the Mayflower Compact quarter was based on the reverse of the [[Pilgrim Tercentenary half dollar]], which was issued in 1920 and 1921. The portrait of Washington on the obverse of the Revolutionary War quarter and the portrait of Madison on the obverse of the Constitution quarter were taken from their respective 2007 [[Presidential dollar coins]]. The portrait of Jefferson on the Declaration of Independence quarter was taken from an [[Indian peace medal]] designed by early Mint engravers [[Robert Scot]] and [[John Reich (engraver)|John Reich]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Jewett |first1=Larry |title=2026 Circulating Coins |url=https://www.coinworld.com/news/us-coins/2026-circulating-coins |access-date=July 1, 2026 |work=Coin World |date=December 16, 2025 |language=en}}</ref>

=== Reaction ===
Acting Mint director Kristie McNally stated of the December 2025 designs, "the designs on these historic coins depict the story of America’s journey toward a 'more perfect union', and celebrate America’s defining ideals of liberty,"<ref name = "first" /> According to the Mint, the replacement designs celebrate "American history and the birth of our great nation".<ref>{{cite news |title = Killing the penny was just the start. Trump is rewriting the rules on America's coins |first = Chris |last = Isidore |url = https://www.cnn.com/2025/12/12/business/trump-changes-coins-policy |date = December 12, 2025 |publisher = CNN |access-date = December 13, 2025 |archive-date = December 12, 2025 |archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20251212141835/https://www.cnn.com/2025/12/12/business/trump-changes-coins-policy |url-status = live }}</ref> [[United States Treasurer]] [[Brandon Beach]] stated of the design change, "while the Biden administration and Secretary Yellen remained focused on [[DEI]] and [[critical race theory]] policies, the Trump administration is dedicated to fostering prosperity and patriotism."<ref name="week" >{{cite news |first = Alia |last = Shoaib |title = Trump Admin Scraps 'DEI' Coins Honoring Abolition of Slavery, Civil Rights |url = https://www.newsweek.com/trump-admin-scraps-dei-coins-honoring-abolition-slavery-civil-rights-11201895 |date = December 12, 2025 |access-date = December 14, 2025 |journal = [[Newsweek]] |archive-date = December 12, 2025 |archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20251212152329/https://www.newsweek.com/trump-admin-scraps-dei-coins-honoring-abolition-slavery-civil-rights-11201895 |url-status = live }}</ref> [[Catherine Cortez Masto]], a Democratic senator from Nevada, regretted the abandonment of the Biden-era designs. "Those designs represented 'continued progress toward 'a more perfect union'.&nbsp;... The American story didn’t stop at the pilgrims and founding fathers."<ref name = "ap">{{cite news |agency = Associated Press |website = Federal News Network |title = New coins will commemorate 250th anniversary of American independence. Here's how they'll look |last = Cooper |first = Jonathan J. |url = https://federalnewsnetwork.com/management/2025/12/new-coins-will-commemorate-250th-anniversary-of-american-independence-heres-how-theyll-look/ |date = December 12, 2025 |access-date = December 13, 2025 |archive-date = December 13, 2025 |archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20251213012429/https://federalnewsnetwork.com/management/2025/12/new-coins-will-commemorate-250th-anniversary-of-american-independence-heres-how-theyll-look/ |url-status = live }}</ref> 

The original coin designs, according to ''[[Canadian Coin News]]'', "would have gone much further in foregrounding social-justice milestones", and their abandonment provoked debate among coin collectors, officials and the general public.<ref name="Canada">{{cite news |date = December 11, 2025 |title = U.S. semiquincentennial coins revealed |url = https://canadiancoinnews.com/u-s-semiquincentennial-coins-revealed/ |url-status = live |archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20260118141702/https://canadiancoinnews.com/u-s-semiquincentennial-coins-revealed/ |archive-date = January 18, 2026 |access-date = December 14, 2025 |newspaper = Canadian Coin News }}</ref>  ''[[Newsweek]]'' called the change, "the latest example of the Trump administration’s broader effort to reshape how American history is officially depicted".<ref name = "week"/> ''[[The New York Times]]'' titled its article on the announcement, "The war on '[[woke]]ness' comes to the U.S. Mint."<ref name="nyt">{{cite news |last = Barry |first = Dan |date = December 14, 2025 |title = The War on 'Wokeness" Comes to the U.S. Mint |url = https://www.nytimes.com/2025/12/14/us/coins-us-250th-anniversary.html?searchResultPosition=1 |access-date = December 19, 2025 |work = [[The New York Times]] }}</ref> [[Quartz (publication)|''Quartz'']] suggested that the replacement of the recommended designs, "amounts to a Whiter interpretation of American history over 250 years".<ref name = "quartz" />

Patrick A. Heller of ''[[Numismatic News]]'' expressed surprise that the presence of a woman as one of two figures on the obverse of the Mayflower Compact quarter was used to fulfill the statutory requirement that one of the quarters denote the contributions of a woman or women, as only adult men not in servitude drafted and signed the Mayflower Compact.<ref name ="num news">{{cite news |newspaper = Numismatic News |first = Patrick A. |last = Heller |title = Surprises In U.S. Mint's Announced 2026 Circulating Coinage |date = December 19, 2025 |access-date = December 19, 2025 |url = https://www.numismaticnews.net/surprises-in-u-s-mints-announced-2026-circulating-coinage?utm_medium=email&utm_source=NMM+-+NL+-+Friday+Newsletter&oly_enc_id=8964I0011234D9C |archive-date = December 23, 2025 |archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20251223122304/https://www.numismaticnews.net/surprises-in-u-s-mints-announced-2026-circulating-coinage?utm_medium=email&utm_source=NMM+-+NL+-+Friday+Newsletter&oly_enc_id=8964I0011234D9C |url-status = live }}</ref>

== Trump dollar ==
=== Development and controversy ===
[[File:Trumpdollar.jpg|thumb|The controversial dollar coins featuring President [[Donald Trump]]]]
Under the authorizing legislation signed into law in 2021, the Treasury Secretary is allowed to issue a Semiquincentennial one-dollar piece in 2026 only. On October 3, 2025, the Treasury Department issued design concepts for possible use on the $1 coin. These featured President Donald Trump in profile on the obverse, and on the reverse Trump standing with raised fist and the U.S. flag in the background, with the words Fight! Fight! Fight!, evoking his words and actions during the July 2024 [[Attempted assassination of Donald Trump in Pennsylvania|assassination attempt]] on him. The authenticity of the designs was confirmed by U.S. Treasurer Beach.<ref name = "butler" />

Under the authorizing legislation, a head-and-bust image of any person, living or dead, or any depiction of a living person, was not to be used on the reverse of the coins. No such restriction applies to the obverse. Although only tradition forbids a living person from appearing on a U.S. coin, it has rarely happened. President [[Calvin Coolidge]] appeared on the 1926 [[United States Sesquicentennial coinage|Sesquicentennial half dollar]], and, more recently, [[Eunice Kennedy Shriver]] appeared on the 1995 Special Olympics silver dollar.<ref name = "butler">{{cite news |last1 = Unser |first1 = Mike |url = https://www.coinnews.net/2025/10/04/treasury-draft-trump-coin-semiquincentennial/ |title = Treasury Unveils Draft Trump 2026 $1 Coin for Semiquincentennial |date = October 4, 2025 |access-date = December 13, 2025 |work = Coin News |archive-date = November 15, 2025 |archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20251115025321/https://www.coinnews.net/2025/10/04/treasury-draft-trump-coin-semiquincentennial/ |url-status = live }}</ref> An 1866 law forbids placing a living person on U.S. paper currency, following the controversial appearance of [[Bureau of Engraving and Printing|National Currency Bureau]] superintendent [[Spencer M. Clark]] on the five-cent [[fractional currency]] note.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=[[Coin World]]|title=5-cent coin replaces note of same denomination|first=Gerald|last=Tebben|access-date=March 24, 2026|date=January 1, 2017|url=https://www.coinworld.com/news/paper-money/5-cent-coin-replaces-note-of-same-denomination.html}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine|magazine=[[Time (magazine)|Time]]|title=Trump to appear on commemorative gold coin, breaking tradition|first=Miranda|last=Jeyaretnam|date=March 20, 2026|access-date=March 24, 2026|url=https://time.com/article/2026/03/20/trump-gold-coin-250th-anniversary-commemorative-king-living-person-tradition/}}</ref>

[[Richard Blumenthal]] of Connecticut, a Democratic senator, stated that Trump "has no right to put his image on the US currency just by fiat".<ref name="quartz">{{cite news |last = Zeballos-Roig |first = Joseph |date = December 19, 2025 |title = Washington fights about what America's money actually looks like |url = https://www.yahoo.com/news/articles/washington-fights-americas-money-actually-100000952.html?fr=sycsrp_catchall |url-status = live |archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20251222233106/https://www.yahoo.com/news/articles/washington-fights-americas-money-actually-100000952.html?fr=sycsrp_catchall |archive-date = December 22, 2025 |access-date = December 20, 2025 |newspaper = [[Quartz (publication)|Quartz]] |via = Yahoo! News }}</ref> Democratic senators introduced a bill intended to block a Trump dollar, to which Beach responded that Democrats were "so triggered by the proposed coin celebrating our nation’s 250th anniversary that they are trying to recklessly change law to block it."<ref name = "nyt" /> Beach, an advocate of a Trump coin, added, "The American people deserve a commemorative coin celebrating our great nation."<ref name = "nyt" /> 

Some members of the CCAC took the position that a Trump dollar violated the spirit of laws restricting where images of living people, including presidents, could appear on the coinage, though it had voted in favor of the image of Ruby Bridges, a living person, appearing on the quarter. Treasury Secretary Bessent stated on social media, "there is no profile more emblematic for the front of this coin than that of our serving President, Donald J. Trump."<ref name= "times committee"/>

=== Approval and production ===
[[Paul Hollis]], nominated by Trump to be the [[Director of the Mint]], appeared at confirmation hearings on October 30, 2025, and was questioned by [[Chris Van Hollen]], Democratic senator from Maryland, about the legality of a Trump dollar. Hollis told Van Hollen he would consult the proper legal authorities.<ref>{{cite news |first = Paul |last = Gilkes |title = Mint Director nominee Hollis appears at hearing |newspaper = [[Coin World]] |date = November 19, 2025 |url = https://www.coinworld.com/news/us-coins-page/mint-director-nominee-hollis-appears-at-hearing |accessdate = December 21, 2025 |archive-date = December 22, 2025 |archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20251222004734/https://www.coinworld.com/news/us-coins-page/mint-director-nominee-hollis-appears-at-hearing |url-status = live }}</ref> On December 18, Hollis was confirmed by the Senate.<ref>{{cite news |last1 = Unser |first1 = Mike |date = December 19, 2025 |title = Senate Confirms Paul Hollis as 41st U.S. Mint Director |url = https://www.coinnews.net/2025/12/19/senate-confirms-paul-hollis-as-41st-u-s-mint-director/ |archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20260113182654/https://www.coinnews.net/2025/12/19/senate-confirms-paul-hollis-as-41st-u-s-mint-director/ |archive-date = January 13, 2026 |access-date = December 21, 2025 |work = Coin News }}</ref> When other Semiquincentennial designs were announced in December 2025, no statement was made regarding the dollar coin, and government sources declined to comment when asked if it were still planned for President Trump to appear on it.<ref name = "ap" /> Nevertheless, the Mint posted on its website candidate designs for the dollar coin, with all obverses depicting Trump.<ref name = "wsj" /> 

On October 27, 2025, President Trump dismissed all members of the CFA. Every CFA member then serving had been appointed by the previous president, [[Joe Biden]],<ref name = "arts">{{cite news |first = Paul |last = Gilkes |title = Trump dismisses all Commission of Fine Arts members |newspaper = [[Coin World]] |date = October 29, 2025 |url = https://www.coinworld.com/news/us-coins-page/trump-dismisses-all-commission-of-fine-arts-members |accessdate = December 13, 2025 |archive-date = December 13, 2025 |archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20251213170821/https://www.coinworld.com/news/us-coins-page/trump-dismisses-all-commission-of-fine-arts-members |url-status = live }}</ref> who had, in 2021, dismissed the members of the CFA appointed by Trump during his first term as president.<ref>{{cite news |publisher = [[CNN]] |first = Liz |last = Stark |url = https://edition.cnn.com/2021/05/25/politics/white-house-arts-commission |date = May 5, 2021 |accessdate = December 13, 2025 |title = White House ousts four Trump-appointed members of prestigious arts commission |archive-date = December 13, 2025 |archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20251213170821/https://edition.cnn.com/2021/05/25/politics/white-house-arts-commission |url-status = live }}</ref> Trump appointed new members to the CFA, who were sworn in on January 22, 2026.<ref>{{cite news |last1 = Gilkes |first1 = Paul |title = Horse again for Liberty in 2027 |url = https://www.coinworld.com/news/us-coins/horse-again-for-liberty-in-2027|date= February 22, 2026 |access-date = February 25, 2026 |work = [[Coin World]] }}</ref> At the CFA meeting that day, the commission recommended candidate designs for the dollar coin, showing Trump in profile on the obverse and an eagle from the [[Great Seal of the United States]] on the reverse. Megan Sullivan, acting head of the Mint’s Office of Design Management, said legal research by the Mint and Treasury had affirmed the legality of the Trump dollar under the authorizing legislation.<ref name = "recommend" /><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.cfa.gov/records-research/project-search/cfa-22-jan-26-8|title=CFA 22/JAN/26-8|date=January 26, 2026|access-date=February 25, 2026|publisher=[[U.S. Commission of Fine Arts]]}}</ref> 

The Mint added the Trump dollar to the CCAC's agenda for its planned meeting at the end of 2025, as well as a gold coin depicting Trump to the February 24, 2026, CCAC agenda. Both were removed by the chair of the CCAC on behalf of a majority of the membership, and the late-2025 meeting was not held; none was held in January 2026 despite there being coinage designs from multiple programs for the CCAC to review. The Mint took the position that a majority of the CCAC had chosen not to review the Trump dollar, and did not seek to have it considered at the February 24 meeting.<ref name = "recommend" /><ref name = "times committee">{{cite news|journal=[[The New York Times]]|date=February 25, 2026|access-date=February 26, 2026|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/02/25/us/trump-coins.html|title=For America's 250th, Should the Coin of Trump's Realm Be Gold?|last=Barry|first=Dan|url-access=subscription}}</ref> The coin is in a gold-colored base metal alloy, like the [[Sacagawea dollar]]. According to ''Coin News'', the design moved forward to the standard review and approval processes within the Treasury Department.<ref name = "recommend">{{cite news |last1 = Unser |first1 = Mike |date = February 27, 2026 |title = U.S. Mint 2026 Trump $1 Coin Designs Advance in Federal Review |url =https://www.coinnews.net/2026/02/27/us-mint-2026-trump-1-coin-designs-reviewed/ |access-date = February 27, 2025 |work = Coin News }}</ref> Scarinci, by then the CCAC chair, disputed that the CCAC had waived its chance to review the coins and said they would be illegal unless reviewed by the CCAC, but also that the committee would not review them because it was against putting living presidents on coins.<ref name = "times march 19" />

In March 2026, ''The New York Times'' reported that prototypes had been struck both of the $1 coin and of a $250 coin containing an ounce of gold, both to depict Trump, and Secretary Bessent had given preliminary approval.<ref name = "times march 19">{{cite news|title=Trump's Handpicked Arts Commission Approves Gold Coin With His Face on It|newspaper=[[The New York Times]]|date=March 19, 2026|access-date=March 20, 2026|first1=Luke|last1=Broadwater|first2=Charlie|last2= Savage|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/19/us/politics/trump-gold-coin.html}}</ref> Bessent announced the final design of the $1 coin in July 2026, for issuance later in the year.<ref name="u928">{{citation | last1=Vipers | first1=Gareth | last2=Rubin | first2=Richard | title=Trump Dollar 'Gold Coin' to Go Into Production, Bessent Says | date=July 15, 2026 | publisher=The Wall Street Journal | url=https://www.wsj.com/politics/policy/trump-dollar-gold-coin-to-go-into-production-bessent-says-3d91fb03 | language=en-US | access-date=July 15, 2026|url-access=subscription }}</ref> The obverse, based on one of the earlier three designs, shows Trump in a suit and tie, facing almost forward, by the Mint's chief engraver, [[Joseph Menna]]. The reverse, credited to the late chief engraver [[Frank Gasparro]] depicts the [[Great Seal of the United States]]. The coins are struck at Philadelphia without mint mark,<ref name = "commend">{{cite news |last1 = Unser |first1 = Mike |date = July 16, 2026 |title = Treasury Unveils U.S. Mint's 1776-2026 Trump $1 Coin |url =https://www.coinnews.net/2026/07/16/treasury-unveils-trump-1-coin/|access-date = July 17, 2026 |work = Coin News }}</ref> and in addition to sales in rolls and bags to collectors, will be placed in circulation.<ref name = "trump" />

===Release and collecting===
On September 2, 2026, the coins were made available from the Mint. A total of 250,000 of the Trump dollars were struck on July 4 and bear a "JULY 4th" privy mark.<ref>{{cite news|first=Rebecca|last=Schneid|journal=[[Time (magazine)|Time]]|title=U.S. Mint Begins Selling Trump $1 Coins. Are They Legal|url=https://time.com/article/2026/09/02/trump-1-gold-coins-go-on-sale/|date=September 2, 2026|access-date=September 8, 2026}}</ref> These were mixed among the coins sold in bags and rolls, with the total authorized mintage for the coins sold in bags and rolls at 8,750,000. The rolls of Trump dollars and the bags sold out within hours, and were the Mint's top two products in terms of sales for the week of their release.<ref>{{cite news |last1 = Unser |first1 = Mike |date = September 9, 2026 |title = US Mint Sales: Trump Dollars and Palladium Eagle Coins Gone |url =https://www.coinnews.net/2026/09/09/sales-trump-1-palladium-eagle/|access-date = September 9, 2026 |work = Coin News }}</ref> A gold version struck at West Point, with a $250 denomination, was in September announced for a November 5 release.<ref name = "Trump 250">{{cite news |last1 = Unser |first1 = Mike |date = September 11, 2026 |title = U.S. Mint Reveals 1776-2026 $250 Trump Gold Coin |url =https://www.coinnews.net/2026/09/11/mint-reveals-250-trump-gold-coin/|access-date = September 11, 2026 |work = Coin News }}</ref>

== Production and release ==
[[File:SemiQ-Dime-Reverse-Unc.jpg|thumb|Reverse of the 1776~2026 dime, featuring an eagle carrying 13 arrows]]
Production of the 1776~2026 quarters began with the striking of 78.2&nbsp;million Mayflower Compact quarters and one million Revolutionary War quarters in December 2025, with 48.8&nbsp;million of the Mayflower coins and all of the Revolutionary coins struck at the [[Denver Mint]] and the remainder, all Revolutionary War quarters, struck at the [[Philadelphia Mint]].<ref>{{cite news |last1 = Unser |first1 = Mike |date = April 13, 2026 |title = U.S. Mint First-Quarter 2026 Coin Production Falls Nearly 50% |url = https://www.coinnews.net/2026/04/13/mint-first-quarter-coin-production/|access-date=April 15, 2026}}</ref> The Mint announced the release of the Mayflower Compact quarter into circulation on January 5, 2026.<ref name = "flower" /> Also being released for circulation then were the dime and half dollar.<ref name="half">{{cite news |last1 = Unser |first1 = Mike |date = January 16, 2026 |title = U.S. Mint Starts Shipping 1776 ~ 2026 Coins for Circulation |url = https://www.coinnews.net/2026/01/16/shipping-2026-coins-circulation/ |url-status = live |archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20260118141309/https://www.coinnews.net/2026/01/16/shipping-2026-coins-circulation/ |archive-date = January 18, 2026 |access-date = January 16, 2026 |work = Coin News }}</ref> When the Mayflower Compact quarters in rolls and in bags were placed on sale on the Mint's web site on February 5, 2026, they became unavailable within minutes but still were the first-, third- and fourth-best selling numismatic items of the week at the Mint.<ref>{{cite news |last1 = Unser |first1 = Mike |title = US Mint Sales: Mayflower Compact Quarters Sell Fast, Platinum Eagle Ranks Second |url = https://www.coinnews.net/2026/02/10/mayflower-compact-quarters-sell-fast/|date = February 10, 2026 |access-date = February 11, 2026 |work = Coin News }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1 = Unser |first1 = Darrin Lee |title = U.S. Mint Offers 1776-2026 Semiquincentennial Mayflower Compact Quarter Rolls and Bags |url = https://www.coinnews.net/2026/02/05/mint-offers-mayflower-compact-quarter/ |date= February 5, 2026 |access-date = February 11, 2026 |work = Coin News }}</ref>

In February, the Mint published images of the year's proof set and silver proof set, both at substantial price increases from the previous year. The silver proof set includes the 1776~2026 cent, struck with an [[San Francisco Mint|S]] [[mint mark]], as does the proof set, which is planned to be released later in the year. Both sets include a [[Native American dollar]] for 2026. According to ''Coin News'', subscriptions which would entitle the holder to purchase each set showed as not available on the Mint's website.<ref>{{cite news |last1 = Unser |first1 = Mike |title = U.S. Mint Reveals 2026 Proof and Silver Proof Sets With Semiquincentennial Coins |url = https://www.coinnews.net/2026/02/16/mint-reveals-2026-sets/|date= February 16, 2026 |access-date = February 24, 2026 |work = Coin News }}</ref>

In March 2026, there were some comments on the eagle on the reverse of the dime, which carries 13 arrows, but not, as on the [[Great Seal of the United States]], an olive branch. In February 2026, the designer, Eric David Custer, stated that the design was a remembrance of the Americans at the time of the Revolutionary War, who were still waiting for peace.<ref>{{cite web|work=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]]|first=Addy|last=Bink|title=Why the newest dimes are missing a symbol of peace in the US|url=https://thehill.com/homenews/nexstar_media_wire/5785001-why-the-newest-dimes-are-missing-a-symbol-of-peace-in-the-us/|date=March 15, 2026|accessdate=March 15, 2026}}</ref> The design with the arrows was reviewed and recommended by the CCAC in July 2024, before Trump won election to a second term.<ref>{{cite news|title=A symbol of peace was cut from US dimes. Is it a message from Trump?|newspaper=[[USA TODAY]]|date=March 14, 2026|first=Jeanine|last=Santucci}}</ref>

The Revolutionary War quarter entered circulation on March 23, 2026, with a launch event at Washington's home, [[Mount Vernon]].<ref name = "war" /> When released as a Mint product four days later, the rolls and bags sold out almost immediately, and were the three top-selling products for the Mint that week. The same week, the Mayflower Compact coins returned to sale and were the fourth through sixth best-selling products of the week, as the Mint exceeded originally stated product limits.<ref>{{cite news |last1 = Unser |first1 = Mike |title = US Mint Sales: Revolutionary War Quarters Vanish in a Flash |url = https://www.coinnews.net/2026/04/01/revolutionary-war-quarters-vanish/|date= April 1, 2026 |access-date = April 1, 2026 |work = Coin News }}</ref> Placed on sale in rolls and bags at a premium on April 17, the Emerging Liberty dime sold out within minutes, becoming the week's top seller for the Mint.<ref>{{cite news |last1 = Unser |first1 = Mike |title = US Mint Sales: 1776-2026 Dime Debuts on Top |url = https://www.coinnews.net/2026/04/22/sales-2026-dime-top/|date= April 22, 2026 |access-date = April 22, 2026 |work = Coin News }}</ref>

On May 15, 2026, the Mint sent a 1776~2026 nickel, a dime, a half dollar and the set of five quarters into space on a [[SpaceX Falcon 9]] to the [[International Space Station]]. The coins were part of the payload of [[NASA]]'s 34th SpaceX [[Commercial Resupply Services]] mission ([[SpaceX CRS-34]]). Mint Director Hollis attended the launch of the uncrewed craft.<ref>{{cite news |last1 = Unser |first1 = Mike |title = U.S. Mint Sends 2026 Semiquincentennial Coins to Space |url = https://www.coinnews.net/2026/05/16/semiquincentennial-coins-to-space/|date= May 15, 2026 |access-date = May 17, 2026 |work = Coin News }}</ref>

The Declaration of Independence quarter entered circulation on May 25, 2026.<ref name = "declaration" /> On June 23, Hollis announced that 250,000 Declaration quarters bearing no mint mark but with a July 4 [[privy mark]], would be hidden among the Declaration quarters shipped to banks for the public to find.<ref name = "hide" >{{cite news |last1 = Unser |first1 = Mike |title = U.S. Mint Producing 250,000 July 4 Privy Mark Quarters |url = https://www.coinnews.net/2026/06/23/us-mint-july-4-quarters/ |date= June 23, 2026 |access-date = June 23, 2026 |work = Coin News }}</ref> The Mint further announced, on July 7, that it had struck on July 4 a total of 250 coins bearing the Declaration design and July 4 privy mark containing 2.5 troy ounces of silver. The coins are plain edged, and sequentially numbered from 1 to 250, and are to be sold at auction.<ref name = "big" >{{cite news |last1 = Unser |first1 = Mike |title =U.S. Mint Strikes Giant Silver Declaration of Independence Quarters |url = https://www.coinnews.net/2026/07/07/us-mint-giant-quarters/ |date= July 7, 2026 |access-date = July 8, 2026 |work = Coin News }}</ref>

The silver proof set went on sale on June 11. This included the dime, quarters and half dollar in silver, plus a [[Native American dollar]], nickel and the first 1776~2026 cent, with all coins bearing a [[San Francisco Mint|S]] [[mint mark]]. After several days, it was selling on [[eBay]] at a premium.<ref name = "silver" >{{cite news |last1 = Unser |first1 = Mike |title = U.S. Mint Silver Set With 1776-2026 Cent Draws High Premiums |url = https://www.coinnews.net/2026/06/21/mint-silver-set-draws-premiums/|date= June 21, 2026 |access-date = June 24, 2026 |work = Coin News }}</ref> The uncirculated sets went on sale on June 30, containing coins from Philadelphia and Denver, including the only cents from those mints. A total of 300,000 were to be issued; they sold out in about 24 hours.<ref name = "mint set" >{{cite news |last1 = Unser |first1 = Mike |title = 2026 Mint Set Premiums Surge Behind Scarce 1776-2026 Cents |url = https://www.coinnews.net/2026/07/06/mint-set-premiums//|date= July 6, 2026 |access-date = July 8, 2026 |work = Coin News }}</ref>

In August, the Mint presented 2,983 half dollars to the [[National 9/11 Memorial and Museum]]. These were struck at the West Point Mint, one for every person listed on the memorial, and bear a commemorative privy mark.<ref name = "point">{{cite news |last1 = Unser |first1 = Mike |title = U.S. Mint Strikes 2,983 9/11 Privy Half Dollars |url = https://www.coinnews.net/2026/08/21/mint-9-11-privy-half-dollars/|date = August 21, 2026 |access-date = August 23, 2026}}</ref> The following month, the Mint announced that 250,000 examples of the 1776~2026–W half dollar with that privy mark would be released into circulation.<ref name = "lesser">{{cite news |last1 = Unser |first1 = Mike |title = 250,000 W-Mint 9/11 Half Dollars Released Into Circulation |url = https://www.coinnews.net/2026/09/14/w-mint-9-11-half-dollars-circulation/|date= September 14, 2026 |access-date = September 14, 2026 |work = Coin News }}</ref> Also in September, the Mint announced the sale of more half dollars with a different privy mark marking 9/11 and struck at Philadelphia.<ref name = "less">{{cite news |last1 = Unser |first1 = Mike |title = U.S. Mint Offers Limited-Time 9/11 Privy Half Dollars |url = https://www.coinnews.net/2026/09/10/mint-limited-time-9-11-privy-half-dollars/|date= September 10, 2026 |access-date = September 10, 2026 |work = Coin News }}</ref>

== {{visible anchor|Best of the Mint}} reproductions; other issues ==
[[File:National Portrait Gallery Portrait of President Donald J Trump 2026 (HR).jpg|thumb|upright|alt=A man in a suit and tie stands looking into the camera, his fists resting on his desk|Photograph of Trump by [[Daniel Torok]] on which the commemorative dollar coin is based]]
In February 2024, the Mint announced plans, based on public surveys, to issue one-ounce and fractional ounce reproductions of classic coin issues during 2026, known as the "Best of the Mint" program. It announced five candidates, which gained immediate support from the CCAC: the 1916 [[Mercury dime]], [[Standing Liberty quarter]] and [[Walking Liberty half dollar]], the 1907 [[Saint-Gaudens double eagle]] in high relief, and the [[1804 dollar]]. Each would be struck in gold, bear the Liberty Bell/250 privy mark and be accompanied by a silver medal of modern design inspired by the coin in question.<ref>{{cite news |last1 = Unser |first1 = Mike |title = 'Best of the Mint' 2026 Semiquincentennial Gold Coins Revealed |url = https://www.coinnews.net/2024/03/01/best-of-the-mint-2026-semiquincentennial-gold-coins-revealed/ |date = March 1, 2024 |access-date = December 13, 2025 |work = Coin News |archive-date = December 13, 2025 |archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20251213183515/https://www.coinnews.net/2024/03/01/best-of-the-mint-2026-semiquincentennial-gold-coins-revealed/ |url-status = live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1 = Gilkes |first1 = Paul |title = CFA takes different approach on 2026 medal design |url = https://www.coinworld.com/news/us-coins/cfa-takes-different-approach-on-2026-medal-design |access-date = December 12, 2025 |work = Coin World |date = July 27, 2024 |archive-date = December 18, 2025 |archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20251218010618/https://www.coinworld.com/news/us-coins/cfa-takes-different-approach-on-2026-medal-design |url-status = live }}</ref>

The [[American Silver Eagle]] coins were struck with the privy mark and dual dating, excepting the pieces at the [[Philadelphia Mint]] for the Congratulations Set, which carry only the 2026 date and no privy mark.<ref>{{cite news |last1 = Unser |first1 = Mike |title = 2026 Congratulations Set Features Low-Mintage Philly Proof Silver Eagle |url = https://www.coinnews.net/2026/02/24/congratulations-philly-proof-silver-eagle/|date = February 24, 2026 |access-date = March 20, 2026 |work = Coin News}}</ref> The [[American Gold Eagle]] similarly features dual dating and a privy mark.<ref>{{cite news |last1 = Unser |first1 = Darrin Lee |title =U.S. Mint Releases 2026-W Proof Gold Eagles Featuring 1776-2026 Dual Dates |url = https://www.coinnews.net/2026/03/05/mint-releases-proof-gold-eagles-dual-dates/|date = March 5, 2026 |access-date = March 20, 2026 |work = Coin News}}</ref> 

Pieces such as the [[Morgan dollar|Morgan]] and [[Peace dollar|Peace]] dollar issues, and the [[American Buffalo (coin)|American Buffalo]] gold coins  struck during 2026 also bear the 1776~2026 double date and privy mark.

On March 19, 2026, the CFA approved a design for a gold coin commemorating the semiquincentennial, showing Trump leaning over his desk. This issue would be in addition to the proposed Trump dollar coin, not part of the issues authorized by the 2021 act, and no law forbids it.<ref name = "cable" /> The design is based on a photograph of Trump by [[Daniel Torok]] that was placed in the [[Smithsonian National Portrait Gallery]].<ref>{{cite news|publisher = [[Reuters]]|title=Trump-appointed arts panel approves gold coin featuring president's image|first=Tim|last=Reid|date=March 19, 2026|accessdate=March 21, 2026|url=https://www.reuters.com/world/us/trump-appointed-arts-panel-approves-gold-coin-featuring-presidents-image-2026-03-19/}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|first=Darlene|last=Superville|title=Trump commemorative gold coin approved for U.S. Mint to produce for America's 250th|date=March 19, 2026|accessdate=April 18, 2026|publisher=[[Associated Press]]|via=[[PBS]]|url=https://www.pbs.org/newshour/politics/trump-commemorative-gold-coin-approved-for-u-s-mint-to-produce-for-americas-250th}}</ref> Commission member [[Chamberlain Harris]] stated, "It's a very strong and very tough image of him, and I think it's fitting to have a current sitting president who's presiding over the country over the 250th year on a commemorative coin for said year."<ref name = "cable">{{cite news|publisher=[[CNN]]|title=Commemorative Trump coin should be 'as large as possible,' key commission says in granting design approval|last=Klein|first=Betsy|url=https://www.cnn.com/2026/03/19/politics/liberty-coin-trump-size|access-date=March 20, 2026|date=March 19, 2026}}</ref> Members were told by Sullivan that Trump had personally approved the proposed coin's design, and they urged her to make the coin as large as possible to match his preference for bigness.<ref name = "times march 19" /> In May, the Mint disclosed that up to 47 such coins would be issued, at some point after July 4, 2026, and each would contain about $90,000 worth of gold.<ref>{{cite news|title=Trump coins won't be ready for America's 250th, US Mint document shows|newspaper=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]]|date=May 19, 2026|access-date=May 23, 2026|last=Fields|first=Ashleigh|url=https://thehill.com/homenews/administration/5886303-trump-gold-coins-delay-250-anniversary/}}</ref>

In June 2026, the Mint announced that 2,026 $250 pieces, shaped like the Liberty Bell and each containing one troy ounce of gold, would be struck, along with the same number of half-ounce $125 coins and one-half ounce silver medals with the same shape. The $250 is priced at $19,000, the $125 at $10,050 and the silver medal at $750, to go on sale July 16.<ref name = "bell">{{cite news |last1 = Unser |first1 = Mike |title =U.S. Mint Liberty Bell-Shaped Coins and Medal Launch July 16 |url = https://www.coinnews.net/2026/06/16/us-mint-liberty-bell-coins-medal/|date = June 16, 2026 |access-date = June 17, 2026 |work = Coin News}}</ref>

